  "body": "**Stacked on #476** (#339 slice 1, fixture mesh). Slice 3 of #339 per the triage on the issue: posed, rotary and XZ/YZ-arc moves read against the fixture mesh. Slice 1 left those on the box verdict. As before, **the mesh only ever clears a hit the box law flagged; it never adds one.**\n\n## What changed\n**Frames.**\n- **Path frame:** steel that turns with the part, and any steel when no rotary axis is stated (the box laws' frame).\n- **Machine frame, A rotary:** fixed steel under an A rotary op, carried per stretch by `posed_steel::upright_turn`, the same frame `workholding` uses. A pose the rotary alone can't stand upright keeps the box verdict.\n- **Machine frame, spindle C:** each move's C line is recovered from its two stated C angles. Fixed steel gets one exact sweep of the machine's straight station run. Steel turning with the part is read along the real spiral, in stretches turned by −C.\n- **XZ/YZ arcs:** chorded in their own plane, with the same sagitta pad as XY arcs.\n- **Left to the box:**\n  - a C move whose start C isn't the previous move's;\n  - a non-horizontal C pose;\n  - a C turn under 1e-4 rad or near 180°;\n  - a held C under fixed steel;\n  - fixed steel under both a rotary axis and C;\n  - an arc on a C move.\n\n**Why a \"clear\" is safe.**\n1. Each posed move is split into stretches by its pose turn (`midpose::turn_intervals`). Over a stretch the tip stays on its segment within the chord pad, and the pose stays within `swing` of the stretch axis, by midpose's existing rate bound.\n2. A band point `p(t) + Q(t)y` stays within `swing·hypot(max(|lo|,|hi|), r)` of the unturned band. So the band lies inside segment ⊕ oriented cylinder ⊕ ball(pad), a convex set.\n3. GJK sweeps the segment and cylinder exactly, and the ball goes into the margin. \"Clear\" is returned only with a separating-plane proof. A non-finite input or NaN margin counts as a hit.\n4. A turn into another frame adds `dth·ρmax`, with ρmax taken at the stretch's four parallelogram corners plus r + pad. Distance to a line is convex, so that bounds it.\n5. Consecutive stretches overlap at their shared instant, so one inside-the-solid point per band decides. Using an oriented cylinder rather than a capsule means a tilted tool isn't falsely convicted on a vise floor below its tip.\n\n**Labels.** A mesh-kept rapid hit names a band above the flute by its part (Shank, Holder, Spindle), like #464's law (d2). This now applies on the posed and C paths too.\n\n`docs/decisions/339-mesh-posed.md`, including a finding filed as **#486**: `cl::stamp_c` pieces keep the whole move's tool axis, so the box laws may misjudge fixed steel on C turns. The mesh can't fix that, since it only clears hits.\n\n## How tested\n- **New sim `fixture_mesh_posed`** (9 tests, plus 1 heavy-lane):\n  - a tilted pass, an in-place index and a tilted rapid down a vise gap are cleared, while the same index into a jaw and a rapid swung into the other jaw still convict;\n  - A-rotary fixed and turning steel, each with a clear case and a convicting case;\n  - C turns with turning and fixed steel; a move with no prior C, and an axial tool, keep the box verdict;\n  - XZ arcs, one clear and one dipping 0.21 mm into the base; a YZ arc in the gap is clear;\n  - a posed rapid kept by the mesh names its band;\n  - both turn signs (A and C) pinned by mutation.\n- **New app `fixture_mesh_posed`** (2 tests): a 3+2 face indexed B 10° between closed jaws. The box law alone convicts every move, yet the post and generation are clean. With the jaws 1 mm into reach, the post is blocked and generation warns.\n- **One slice-1 pin renamed** (`unpaired_boxes_keep_the_box_verdict_and_posed_moves_are_read`): a posed gap pass is now cleared.\n- **Existing suites keep their verdicts:** posed_fixture, posed_fixture_midpose, posed_rapids, midmove_pose, fixture_stack_span, sim lib; app fixtures, rotary_fixtures, fixture_mesh, fixture_law_gaps. Their fixtures are box-only or open, so the mesh never judges them.\n- **Performance:**\n  - debug, 29.7k facets and 80 posed moves: 0.07 s;\n  - release heavy lane, 203k facets and 2000 posed moves: 0.33 s.\n- `clippy -p camcraft-sim -p camcraft-app --tests -D warnings` is clean.\n\n## Not exercised\nNo GUI or G-code change. More 4/5-axis and rotary programs now post: those whose only \"hit\" was a box around empty space between jaws.\n\nChangelog: Fixture collisions on tilted, rotary and spindle-C moves and XZ/YZ arcs are judged against the fixture's real mesh too, so a tilted pass between vise jaws no longer refuses the post (#339)\n\nRefs #339\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
